 \section*{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
 
-RankCloak makes the representation of cryptographic artifacts an explicit design choice in language-model information hiding. It combines exact serialization, contextual rank transfer and replay decoding with measurements of recovery, rate, text properties and detection. All 6,480 primary trials recovered the original bytes under the saved-token contract. This establishes a reproducible baseline for comparing how artifact representations influence generated cover text.
+RankCloak makes the representation of cryptographic artifacts an explicit design choice in language model information hiding. It combines exact serialization, contextual rank transfer and replay decoding with measurements of recovery, rate, text properties and detection. All 6,480 primary trials recovered the original bytes under the saved-token contract. This establishes a reproducible baseline for comparing how artifact representations influence generated cover text.
 
 The codec comparison identifies a controllable efficiency tradeoff. Direct subword representations can be compact but have no fixed rank ceiling, whereas ASCII-byte and hex-nibble codecs bound rank magnitude by using more forced positions. Segmentation determines how those positions are distributed across contexts. Larger segments reduce repeated continuation overhead, while the tested lead-ins increased length and worsened likelihood. Separate measurements of forced spans and full messages show how greedy continuations affect complete-message scores without changing payload-bearing tokens or increasing payload capacity.
 
