 \section*{Responsible use and limitations}\label{sec:limitations}
 
-RankCloak is a representation layer for cryptographic artifacts. Encryption and authentication must be supplied by an external cryptographic system under an explicit threat model. The carrier itself provides no confidentiality or cryptographic security guarantee. Exact recovery was demonstrated with preserved tokens and matching configurations. Visible-text and transformed-text results show that reliable edited-text transport remains an open problem. Detector findings apply to the evaluated corpus, and operational deployment, broad multilingual text quality and generalization to arbitrary models or channels remain untested.
-
-The contextual study evaluates coherence and acceptability through blinded judgments from local instruction models. Its outcomes are model-based ratings; no participant ratings were collected. Judges and generators are not fully crossed, greedy controls are reused as a fixed reference set, and the selected payloads exclude the earlier scored cohort. The estimates condition on complete ratings. Missingness sensitivity is reported in Supplementary Note S20. The comparison evaluates delivered messages without isolating a causal boundary effect. Supplementary Note S17 retains both unsuccessful semantic control checks and the complementary likelihood analysis. Table S17 and Notes S16 through S20 give the detailed scope of the evidence.
+The carrier itself provides no confidentiality or cryptographic security guarantee. Exact recovery was demonstrated with preserved tokens and matching configurations. Visible text and transformed text results show that reliable edited text transport remains an open problem. 
+The contextual study evaluates coherence and acceptability through blinded judgments from local instruction models. Its outcomes are model-based ratings and missingness sensitivity is reported in Supplementary Note S20. The comparison evaluates delivered messages without isolating a causal boundary effect. Supplementary Note S17 retains both unsuccessful semantic control checks and the complementary likelihood analysis. Table S17 and Notes S16 through S20 give the detailed scope of the evidence.
 
 
 
 
